# Lets the interface's worker run the fourteen bounded mutations, and nothing else.
#
# Its own rule, for the same reason the poller has one. %haproxy-mgmt holds the
# operators and %sudo holds the humans; a service account must not reach a
# privilege because somebody else needed it. This is the separate decision.
#
# What it grants, exactly: fourteen helpers, each with no arguments at all. The
# helpers accept no argv and read a bounded JSON request on stdin, which is what
# makes an exact rule possible - PRIVILEGE.md forbids wildcards in argument
# matching, and a rule naming a cluster would need one.
#
# These fourteen write. That is the point of them, and it is why the list is this
# short: deploy brings nodes onto the published version, cert-deploy distributes
# certificates, revert moves the cluster back to an earlier one, and the two
# publish helpers write the network description and the objects into the cluster
# repository. Everything else the worker might plausibly want - config edits,
# history reset - is absent on purpose.
#
# The last seven are Phase 11's, and they are the ones to read twice, because
# they are the only rules here that reach private key material. What limits them
# is not this file but their own shape: issue obtains a certificate for a domain
# with certbot parameters fixed in the engine, publish distributes what the
# registry already lists, and target changes which registered targets a
# registered certificate goes to, and DNS profile apply materialises or removes
# one database-bound profile in its fixed policy directory. None can name an arbitrary path, account
# or a directory. Registering a receiver - an account, a directory, a lock - is
# not here either: that helper writes /etc, which this unit's sandbox keeps
# read-only, so it answers on a socket from a unit of its own (B163) rather
# than through a rule that could not work.
#
# The tenth, cert-delete, is the one rule here that removes key material, and
# it is bounded the same way: the request names registered certificate IDs
# and nothing else; which of them may go is decided from the registry, the
# cluster files and each cluster's committed objects, never from the request.
# A certificate a committed object still names, a cluster's default, or a
# cluster's last certificate is refused row by row.
#
# What no rule here grants, and no rule ever should: reading a private key. The
# helpers write key material to targets and never return it, which is why the
# caller-matrix test asserts the absence rather than trusting this comment.
#
# Publishing was on that absent list until 2026-09-02, on the reasoning that it
# was an operator's decision made at a terminal. What changed is that the
# interface became where those decisions are made: an editor that can only save
# a draft is an editor whose work has to be finished somewhere else, and asking
# somebody to leave the screen and log in to a shell to complete a change is how
# a change gets left unfinished. Publishing still writes nothing to a node -
# that is deploy, which is a separate helper and a separate decision.
#
# What the rule does NOT decide: which cluster, which version, whether to touch
# any node at all. The caller hands over a bounded request; the privileged half
# reads the cluster's real state and decides the rest. So this
# rule cannot be widened by argument, only by editing this file.
#
# The application's own `management_writes` switch is a separate gate and stays
# off until the phase that opens it. This file grants the capability; it does
# not turn writes on.
#
# NOPASSWD because a service has no terminal to type a password at.
#
# Installed 0440 root:root, and accepted by visudo before it is put in place.

WORKER_USER ALL=(root) NOPASSWD: /opt/haprion/current/bin/haprion-deploy-helper ""
WORKER_USER ALL=(root) NOPASSWD: /opt/haprion/current/bin/haprion-cert-deploy-helper ""
WORKER_USER ALL=(root) NOPASSWD: /opt/haprion/current/bin/haprion-revert-helper ""
WORKER_USER ALL=(root) NOPASSWD: /opt/haprion/current/bin/haprion-changes-publish-helper ""
WORKER_USER ALL=(root) NOPASSWD: /opt/haprion/current/bin/haprion-cert-issue-helper ""
WORKER_USER ALL=(root) NOPASSWD: /opt/haprion/current/bin/haprion-cert-publish-helper ""
WORKER_USER ALL=(root) NOPASSWD: /opt/haprion/current/bin/haprion-cert-target-helper ""
WORKER_USER ALL=(root) NOPASSWD: /opt/haprion/current/bin/haprion-dns-profile-apply-helper ""
WORKER_USER ALL=(root) NOPASSWD: /opt/haprion/current/bin/haprion-cert-delete-helper ""

# NDES profiles: ciphertext arrives only on stdin.
WORKER_USER ALL=(root) NOPASSWD: /opt/haprion/current/bin/haprion-ndes-profile-apply-helper ""

# NDES enrollment: the challenge is read from the root-only profile record, never from the request.
WORKER_USER ALL=(root) NOPASSWD: /opt/haprion/current/bin/haprion-cert-scep-helper ""

# Certificate upload (B165): the file, sealed for root, arrives only on stdin.
WORKER_USER ALL=(root) NOPASSWD: /opt/haprion/current/bin/haprion-cert-import-helper ""

# Signing request (C9b): the key is made in the import store and never leaves it; the CSR comes back.
WORKER_USER ALL=(root) NOPASSWD: /opt/haprion/current/bin/haprion-cert-request-helper ""

# Drain, maintenance and ready (B263): one object's servers on every node, found in the newest version first.
WORKER_USER ALL=(root) NOPASSWD: /opt/haprion/current/bin/haprion-server-state-helper ""
